---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The Grave"
  eyebrow="Senj, Crikvenica, Klenovica"
  dek="Six names on one family plot — and one of them belongs to a man who is not buried in it. Two further graves, found since, say something consistent about what a stone is good for and what it is not."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4503429-blazevic-grave.jpg")}
           alt="The Blažević family grave at Senj" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The Blažević plot, Senj. Neighbouring stones read VUKELIĆ, TOMLJANOVIĆ, BEZJAK
      and ROGIĆ — the same surnames that fill this tree.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is cut into it</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Upper stone</th><td>
        <strong>LJUBOMIR BLAŽEVIĆ</strong> ✳ 1891 † 1966<br />
        <strong>MILKA BLAŽEVIĆ</strong> <em>rod.</em> PAPIĆ ✳ 1886 † 1971<br />
        <strong>OTO BLAŽEVIĆ</strong> ✳ 1923 † 1945<br />
        <strong>MARIO BLAŽEVIĆ</strong> ✳ 1933 † 1984</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Later plaque</th><td><strong>BLAŽEVIĆ</strong> —
        <strong>KATA</strong> ✳ 1924 † 2007 · <strong>LEO</strong> ✳ 1920 † 2008</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><em>rod.</em> — <em>rođena</em>, “born”, the Croatian
    equivalent of <em>née</em>. The stone therefore records Milka's <strong>maiden</strong> name,
    Papić — and not <a href={u("/marriage-1920")}>the Vukelić name she married under in
    1920</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why this photograph matters more than any other here</div>
    <h2>It corroborates the account of Oto's death</h2>
    <p class="lede"><strong>OTO BLAŽEVIĆ ✳ 1923 † 1945</strong> is on this stone. The family record
    carries <a href={u("/oto")}>two irreconcilable accounts</a> of how he died — a snakebite, and
    the war in Slavonia — and the second one, from <strong>Nives Blažević</strong>, said this:</p>
    <blockquote class="note">…he died in the war somewhere in Slavonia, his grave is unknown, so
    they put his name in his grandmother's and grandfather's grave — <strong>and now Leo is there,
    and Katica</strong>.</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">The stone shows <strong>exactly that</strong>. Oto's name is on the
    family plot; and the later plaque below carries <strong>LEO</strong> (1920–2008) and
    <strong>KATA</strong> — <em>Katica</em> — (1924–2007), added afterwards, precisely as she
    described.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">One wrinkle resolves easily. Nives calls it “his grandmother's and
    grandfather's grave”, but the names on it are Oto's <em>parents</em>. Nives Blažević was born in
    <strong>1953</strong>: Ljubomir and Milka were <em>her</em> grandparents. She is describing the
    grave from her own position in the family, which is what people do.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>What this does and does not prove.</strong> It does not prove he died
    in Slavonia. It proves that <strong>the person who said so knew this grave in detail</strong> —
    including who was added to it half a century later — and that the family commemorated Oto
    somewhere other than a grave of his own. That is real corroboration of the witness, and it moves
    the snakebite account into second place. <a href={u("/oto")}>The brother →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And it contradicts the register</h2>
    <p>The stone gives Ljubomir's birth as <strong>1891</strong>. The
    <a href={u("/marriage-1920")}>1920 marriage register</a>, written in his lifetime from his
    baptismal certificate, gives <strong>1 March 1892</strong>. This archive follows the register.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">It is a useful reminder in a family whose evidence is mostly stone
    and memory: <strong>a gravestone is a secondary source</strong>. It is cut after the fact, paid
    for by the bereaved, and carved by someone who never met the person.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Two more graves, and the same lesson twice</h2>
    <p class="lede">When that last paragraph was written it rested on one stone. Sweeping the
    family's photo library turned up two more, in two more cemeteries — and one of them repeats the
    error exactly, while the other corrects the tree.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <figure class="doc">
        <img src={u("/photos/line/4503717-crikvenica-gravestone-ana-de-franceschi-born-kal.jpg")}
             alt="The Defranceschi family grave at Crikvenica" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption><strong>Crikvenica.</strong> ANA DE. FRANCESCHI ✻19 IX 1863 ✝28 V 1936, her
        husband JOSIP, RUŽA JARMACKI rođena DEFRANCESCHI 1903–1939, and PEPE, TONI and BOŽICA
        below.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="doc">
        <img src={u("/photos/line/4507818-kalanj-grave-klenovica-lovro-1906-1965-ana-1919-.jpg")}
             alt="The Kalanj grave at Klenovica" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption><strong>Klenovica.</strong> LOVRO KALANJ 1906–1965, ANA KALANJ 1919–1971, IVAN
        KALANJ 1942–1996, MARIJAN KALANJ 1944–2009 — a father, a mother and two sons.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>Crikvenica gets a birth date wrong, exactly as Senj does</h3>
      <p>The stone gives <strong>Ana de Franceschi, born 19 September 1863</strong>. Her baptism
      survives and does not agree: <em>Anno Domini 1864</em>, no. 2 — <em>Anna, legitima, Josephus
      Kalanj et Rosalia nata Perković ejus uxor</em>, at <span class="mono">Klenovica N° 22</span>,
      <strong>born 3 January 1864</strong> and baptised three days later. The death on the stone,
      28 May 1936, is exactly right; the birth is out by three and a half months.</p>
      <p><strong>So both stones this archive can test get the birth wrong and the death right</strong>
      — which makes sense. The death is what the family had just lived through. The birth is what
      somebody remembered, seventy years later.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>Every name on the Crikvenica stone is in the tree</h3>
      <p>Read against the record it resolves into one household over three generations, and the
      dates agree throughout:</p>
      <table class="reg">
        <thead><tr><th>On the stone</th><th>In the tree</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">ANA DE. FRANCESCHI ✻19 IX 1863 ✝28 V 1936</td>
            <td><a href={u("/people/anica-ana-anna-kalanj")}>Ana Kalanj</a>, 3 Jan 1864 – 28 May 1936</td>
            <td class="dim">born Kalanj of Klenovica 22 — <strong>the birth date on the stone is wrong</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">JOSIP DE. FRANCESCHI</td><td>Josephus “Josip” de Franceschi</td>
            <td class="dim">her husband</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">RUŽA JARMACKI rođena DEFRANCESCHI ✻1903 ✝1939</td>
            <td>Ruža de Franceschi, 10 Dec 1903 – 29 Mar 1939, m. Miro Jarmacky</td>
            <td class="dim">their daughter</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">ANT… 1890 · URSULA 1895</td>
            <td>Ante Defranceski, b. 20 Jan 1890, and Ursula Kovačina, 1895–1975</td>
            <td class="dim">their son and his wife</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">PEPE 1911–1980 · BOŽICA 1911–1994</td>
            <td>Josip Petar “Pepe” Defranceski, 1911–1980, and Božica Blašković</td>
            <td class="dim">grandson and his wife</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">TONI 1937–1984</td><td>Anton “Braco” Defranceski, 16 Nov 1937 – 17 Jan 1984</td>
            <td class="dim">great-grandson</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Only one of the seven is published here.</strong> This
      archive publishes nine surname groups and Defranceski is not among them, so the stone is
      almost entirely the <em>sister</em> archive's material — reached, as it happens, through a
      Kalanj girl from Klenovica who married into it.</p>
      <p class="dek"><strong>And reading it caught a fault in the tree.</strong> Božica Blašković is
      entered as born <span class="mono">3 December 1911</span> and dying <span class="mono">18
      May</span> — the same day and month as her husband Pepe, in both cases. Two people do not
      share a birthday and a death-day. Her <em>years</em>, 1911 and 1994, match the stone; the days
      and months have been copied across from his record. She is not published here, so this is
      noted rather than corrected — but it is worth the sister archive's attention.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>Klenovica corrects the tree instead</h3>
      <p>The four names on the Kalanj stone are all in this archive — and
      <strong>all four are in it twice</strong>. Because the duplicated parents are spelled
      <em>Lovro</em> in one copy and <em>Lovre</em> in the other, the duplicate check rejected the
      two sons as different people. The photograph is what overruled it: one family, one plot, four
      names. <a href={u("/duplicates")}>The same person, twice →</a></p>
      <p class="dek">A stone is a poor witness to a birth and a good witness to a household. It was
      cut by people who knew exactly who was buried together.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
